		<title>Madonna’s 10 Greatest Songs, according to MOJO.</title>
		<link>http://drownedmadonna.com/madonnas-10-greatest-songs-according-mojo/20150129</link>
		<comments>http://drownedmadonna.com/madonnas-10-greatest-songs-according-mojo/20150129#comments</comments>
		<pubDate>Thu, 29 Jan 2015 17:56:03 +0000</pubDate>
		<dc:creator><![CDATA[Dexter]]></dc:creator>
				<category><![CDATA[magazines]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Mojo magazine]]></category>

		<guid isPermaLink="false">http://drownedmadonna.com/?p=3831</guid>
		<description><![CDATA[<p>MOJO Magazine ranked Madonna&#8216;s 10 Greatest Songs immersing themselves in all her discography and realized that Madonna has a lot [...]</p>
<p>The post <a rel="nofollow" href="http://drownedmadonna.com/madonnas-10-greatest-songs-according-mojo/20150129">Madonna’s 10 Greatest Songs, according to MOJO.</a> appeared first on <a rel="nofollow" href="http://drownedmadonna.com">Madonna news new album Rebel Heart</a>.</p>
]]></description>
				<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p><a href="http://www.mojo4music.com/18740/madonna-10-greatest-songs/" target="_blank">MOJO Magazine</a> ranked <strong>Madonna</strong>&#8216;s <strong>10 Greatest Songs</strong> immersing themselves in all her discography and realized that <strong>Madonna has a lot of great songs</strong> and that for a predominantly dance-pop artist, she is uncommonly <strong>artful</strong> and <strong>eclectic</strong>.<br />
Check out their top 10:</p>
<p><strong>10. Madonna – Hung Up (from Confessions On A Dancefloor, 2005)</strong></p>
<p><span class='embed-youtube' style='text-align:center; display: block;'><iframe class='youtube-player' type='text/html' width='960' height='570' src='http://www.youtube.com/embed/EDwb9jOVRtU?version=3&#038;rel=1&#038;fs=1&#038;showsearch=0&#038;showinfo=1&#038;iv_load_policy=1&#038;wmode=transparent' frameborder='0' allowfullscreen='true'></iframe></span></p>
<p>Madonna’s ability to spot the coming man on the dance scene was never better illustrated than in her co-option of Les Rhythmes Digitales’ Stuart Price. Cue this scintillating disco reboot and what Pete Paphides describes in the current MOJO magazine as “a sublime vocal of unsated longing”.</p>
<p><strong>9. Vogue (single, 1990)</strong></p>
<p><span class='embed-youtube' style='text-align:center; display: block;'><iframe class='youtube-player' type='text/html' width='960' height='570' src='http://www.youtube.com/embed/WhOr2JlhFTI?version=3&#038;rel=1&#038;fs=1&#038;showsearch=0&#038;showinfo=1&#038;iv_load_policy=1&#038;wmode=transparent' frameborder='0' allowfullscreen='true'></iframe></span></p>
<p>In the great tradition of dance craze hits – The Locomotion, The Crusher et al – Madonna delivers in spades, her ode to the short-lived hipster’s semaphore spangled with wide-eyed rapture for the culture of the dancefloor. Strike a pose! There’s nothing to it!</p>
<p><strong>8. Mer Girl (from Ray Of Light, 1998)</strong></p>
<p><span class='embed-youtube' style='text-align:center; display: block;'><iframe class='youtube-player' type='text/html' width='960' height='570' src='http://www.youtube.com/embed/GrFrpWU95-w?version=3&#038;rel=1&#038;fs=1&#038;showsearch=0&#038;showinfo=1&#038;iv_load_policy=1&#038;wmode=transparent' frameborder='0' allowfullscreen='true'></iframe></span></p>
<p>Heartstopping, gob-smacking. This is like a psych horror epic dredged from the depths of Madonna’s subconscious. Her mother’s death evoked, not for the first or last time, as she descends into the grave, ensnared by William Orbit’s spook-house tendrils.</p>
<p><strong>7. Papa Don’t Preach (from True Blue, 1986)</strong></p>
<p><span class='embed-youtube' style='text-align:center; display: block;'><iframe class='youtube-player' type='text/html' width='960' height='570' src='http://www.youtube.com/embed/G333Is7VPOg?version=3&#038;rel=1&#038;fs=1&#038;showsearch=0&#038;showinfo=1&#038;iv_load_policy=1&#038;wmode=transparent' frameborder='0' allowfullscreen='true'></iframe></span></p>
<p>From when Madonna could do effortless, &#8220;issues”-based pop without coming off like a schoolmarm, Papa Don’t Preach drips with empathy and teen-opera drama. And never did strings segue so well into slap bass.</p>
<p><strong>6. What It Feels Like For A Girl (from Music, 2000)</strong></p>
<p><iframe src="//player.vimeo.com/video/43410678" width="640" height="480" frameborder="0" title="Madonna - What It Feels Like For A Girl" webkitallowfullscreen mozallowfullscreen allowfullscreen></iframe></p>
<p>Exquisitely airy, melodic subversion of gender assumptions created in consort with British dance sophisticate Guy Sigsworth (Seal, Björk, Frou Frou) based on samples of avant-guitarist and Sylvian-pal David Torn. This (above) is the trancey video mix; seek out the subtler, superior album version.</p>
<p><strong>5. Justify My Love (single, 1990)</strong></p>
<p><span class='embed-youtube' style='text-align:center; display: block;'><iframe class='youtube-player' type='text/html' width='960' height='570' src='http://www.youtube.com/embed/Np_Y740aReI?version=3&#038;rel=1&#038;fs=1&#038;showsearch=0&#038;showinfo=1&#038;iv_load_policy=1&#038;wmode=transparent' frameborder='0' allowfullscreen='true'></iframe></span></p>
<p>A recurring theme (see Into The Groove, Express Yourself): is he good enough for her/you? But also, Madonna’s most sensual song (before she overdid it with Erotica), drifting druggily on an irresistible breakbeat and post-coital skein of cig-smoke synth. Plus! The best thing Lenny Kravitz ever had a hand in.</p>
<p><strong>4. Into The Groove (single, 1985)</strong></p>
<p><span class='embed-youtube' style='text-align:center; display: block;'><iframe class='youtube-player' type='text/html' width='960' height='570' src='http://www.youtube.com/embed/52iW3lcpK5M?version=3&#038;rel=1&#038;fs=1&#038;showsearch=0&#038;showinfo=1&#038;iv_load_policy=1&#038;wmode=transparent' frameborder='0' allowfullscreen='true'></iframe></span></p>
<p>A distillation of the idea of excitement and one of the greatest pure dancefloor anthems ever, sincerely homaged by Sonic Youth, sincerely envied and admired by everyone else. Still Madonna’s mnaifesto – &#8220;You can dance&#8230; for inspiration&#8221; – and possibly the best synth bass not programmed by David Gamson.</p>
<p><strong>3. Live To Tell (from True Blue, 1986)</strong></p>
<p><span class='embed-youtube' style='text-align:center; display: block;'><iframe class='youtube-player' type='text/html' width='960' height='570' src='http://www.youtube.com/embed/u3qB5RwPQAA?version=3&#038;rel=1&#038;fs=1&#038;showsearch=0&#038;showinfo=1&#038;iv_load_policy=1&#038;wmode=transparent' frameborder='0' allowfullscreen='true'></iframe></span></p>
<p>Madonna’s first great bid to be taken seriously as an artist, Live To Tell is a mini-epic, darkly hinting at childhood horrors amid Pat Leonard’s grand, portentous chimes. Probably her best bit of singing, too, before the voice lessons of Evita opened her bel canto phase.</p>
<p><strong>2. Music (from Music, 2000)</strong></p>
<p><span class='embed-youtube' style='text-align:center; display: block;'><iframe class='youtube-player' type='text/html' width='960' height='570' src='http://www.youtube.com/embed/qi8wJSxCkw8?version=3&#038;rel=1&#038;fs=1&#038;showsearch=0&#038;showinfo=1&#038;iv_load_policy=1&#038;wmode=transparent' frameborder='0' allowfullscreen='true'></iframe></span></p>
<p>A love song to the truest partner in her life, this is the greatest artform as great leveller, its glitchy bits and Flat Beat squelches never in the way of Madonna’s genius for a hook. Adult, eclectic dance music that hasn’t dated one jot.</p>
<p><strong>1. Like A Prayer (from Like A Prayer, 1989)</strong></p>
<p><span class='embed-youtube' style='text-align:center; display: block;'><iframe class='youtube-player' type='text/html' width='960' height='570' src='http://www.youtube.com/embed/rDnUIXF2ly8?version=3&#038;rel=1&#038;fs=1&#038;showsearch=0&#038;showinfo=1&#038;iv_load_policy=1&#038;wmode=transparent' frameborder='0' allowfullscreen='true'></iframe></span></p>
<p>Dangalangalanga&#8230; Boom! What a way to start this extraordinary subversion of the pop ditty, with sensuality and religion bound up in kinky chains but Madonna DEAD SERIOUS about her desperate search for solace. &#8220;Life is a mystery / Everyone must stand alone.” Blimey&#8230;</p>
<p>The post <a rel="nofollow" href="http://drownedmadonna.com/madonnas-10-greatest-songs-according-mojo/20150129">Madonna’s 10 Greatest Songs, according to MOJO.</a> appeared first on <a rel="nofollow" href="http://drownedmadonna.com">Madonna news new album Rebel Heart</a>.</p>
]]></content:encoded>
